This recursive implementation of the Fibonacci sequence has a significant issue: it's highly inefficient and can lead to a stack overflow for large inputs. 

The problem lies in the fact that it performs a large amount of redundant computation. For instance, to calculate `fib(5)`, it will compute `fib(4)` and `fib(3)`. But to compute `fib(4)`, it will again compute `fib(3)`, effectively recalculating `fib(3)` twice. This redundancy increases exponentially with the input size, leading to a time complexity of O(2^n).

Moreover, each recursive call adds a layer to the system call stack. For large inputs, this can cause a stack overflow, as the maximum stack depth is typically limited.

A more efficient approach would be to use dynamic programming, storing previously computed values to avoid redundant calculations. Here's an example in pseudo-code:

```
function fib(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    int[0...n] memo;
    memo[0] = 0;
    memo[1] = 1;

    for (i from 2 to n) {
        memo[i] = memo[i-1] + memo[i-2];
    }
    return memo[n];
}
```

This version has a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(n) as well, but it avoids the stack overflow issue and the redundant calculations.

