The recursive implementation you provided for the Fibonacci sequence has a major issue called "stack overflow error". This is because the function calls itself multiple times, which leads to a large number of function calls being pushed onto the call stack. This can cause the call stack to overflow, resulting in a runtime error.

In the Fibonacci sequence, each number is the sum of the two preceding ones, so the nth number is computed based on the (n-1)th and (n-2)th numbers. In the recursive implementation, we're calling the `fib` function twice to compute the (n-1)th and (n-2)th numbers, which leads to a lot of redundant computations.

Dynamic programming is a technique that can optimize recursive algorithms with overlapping subproblems by storing the results of expensive function calls and returning the cached result when the same inputs occur again. In the case of the Fibonacci sequence, we can use dynamic programming to avoid redundant computations and improve the performance of the algorithm.

Here's an implementation of the Fibonacci sequence using dynamic programming:

```c++
int fib(int n, vector<int>& memo) {
    if (n <= 1) {
        memo[n] = n;
        return n;
    }
    if (memo[n] != -1) {
        return memo[n];
    }
    memo[n] = fib(n-1, memo) + fib(n-2, memo);
    return memo[n];
}

int fibonacci(int n) {
    vector<int> memo(n+2, -1);
    return fib(n, memo);
